[Event "Stafford Gambit: the gambit itself"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/I6bcI5Em"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]

{ Today we learn the Stafford Gambit. You give up a pawn for fast pieces, point everything you own at f2, and set traps he will not see coming. }
1. e4 { His king pawn marches to e4, claiming the centre. Meet it head on: push your own pawn to e5. [%cal Ye7e5] } 1... e5 { Straight back in the centre, pawn for pawn. Nobody has given anything away yet. } (1... c5 { The Sicilian, and a perfectly good answer, but it takes the game somewhere else entirely. } 2. Nf3 d6 { Sharp, famous, and no Stafford anywhere in sight. Today we meet him head on. }) (1... e6 { The French: solid, though that pawn shuts your own light bishop in behind it. } 2. d4 d5 { A patient, blocked sort of game, and we keep a whole study for it. Today we want the open fight. }) (1... c6 { A fine move, and a completely different sort of game. } 2. d4 d5 { That is the Caro-Kann, solid and slow. Today we want the sharp, open kind of fight. }) 2. Nf3 { His knight hits your pawn on e5. Do not stop to defend it: bring your own king knight out instead. [%cal Yg8f6] } 2... Nf6 { The natural developing move, and it hits his pawn on e4 in the very same breath. } (2... Nc6 { Just as good: it defends the pawn the normal way. But it is not the gambit we are learning today. } 3. Bb5 a6 { That is the Ruy Lopez, one of the deepest openings ever played. A story for another day. }) 3. Nxe5 { His knight grabs your pawn. Do not take back: bring your other knight out to c6 and offer it instead. That is the Stafford. [%cal Yb8c6] } 3... Nc6 { Your knight hits his knight straight back. He has to decide what to do with it right now. } (3... d6 { The sound, safe move, and it wins the pawn straight back. But that is the quiet Petrov, not our gambit. } 4. Nf3 Nxe4 5. d4 { Even, correct chess, and nobody ever falls for a single trick in it. }) (3... Nxe4 { Also perfectly good, and perfectly calm. A real option, just not today's plan. } 4. Qe2 Qe7 5. Qxe4 d6 { He takes your knight on e4, but your pawn on d6 hits his knight on e5 and wins the piece back. Sound, and quiet. }) 4. Nxc6 { He takes your knight. Recapture with the d pawn, not the b pawn: the difference matters a lot. [%cal Yd7c6] } 4... dxc6 { The Stafford proper. You are a pawn down, but the d column is open for your queen, your bishop on c8 is free, and every piece has a road toward f2. [%csl Gc8,Gd8] } (4... bxc6 { Also sound, but the pawn on c6 is not the problem: your d pawn stays home and shuts the light bishop in. [%csl Rc8] } 5. Nc3 Bb4 { Playable, and a much quieter afternoon. The d pawn capture opens a road for your queen and bishop at once. }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: oh no, my queen"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/kZFfzTvW"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_0_5"]

{ The Stafford position. He is a pawn up, but your queen, bishops and knight all have open roads, and his pieces are still at home. }
5. d3 { He props his pawn on e4 up with d3. His weakest square is f2, guarded only by his king. Which piece can aim at it right now? } 5... Bc5 { Your bishop lands on c5 and stares straight at f2, the square only his king guards. [%cal Gc5f2] } (5... Be6 { A solid square, but from e6 the bishop looks at nothing near his king. On c5 it would stare straight at f2. } 6. Be2 Qe7 7. f4 { A sound game, and he gets to push his pawns while your bishop sleeps. }) (5... Qe7 { Also good, and she eyes the open e column. But the bishop to c5 comes first: it is the piece that hits f2. } 6. Nc3 Be6 { Perfectly playable, just with no trap waiting for him. }) 6. Bg5 { He pins your knight to your queen, and that is his mistake: bishop to e2, to get castled, was the safe move. Is your knight really pinned? [%csl Rf6] } 6... Nxe4 { It never was a real pin. Your knight grabs e4, and now it hits his bishop on g5 and the pawn on f2. [%cal Ge4g5,Ge4f2] } (6... Bxf2+ { Also winning, and tempting to grab right away. But the queen herself was the bigger prize. } 7. Kxf2 Nxe4+ 8. Ke1 Nxg5 { A pawn and a bishop won, and your queen was never in danger. Good, but there was mate on offer. }) 7. Bxd8 { He grabs your queen, and walks into mate in two. Taking the knight with his d pawn was his better try, still lost but no mate. Find the check. } 7... Bxf2+ { Check! A pawn falls too, and his king has only one square left: e2. } (7... Kxd8 { The natural recapture, and it throws everything away. } 8. dxe4 Bd6 { You are simply a queen down for a bishop. The check on f2 had to come first. }) (7... Nxf2 { The wrong piece takes. Without a check, he gets time to defend. } 8. Qh5 b6 9. Qg5 { His queen swings to g5 and hits g7, and your attack has gone missing, with a queen fewer. }) 8. Ke2 { Forced: e2 is the only square his king has. One more piece, and it is over. } 8... Bg4# { Checkmate! Your bishops on g4 and f2 box his king in on e2, and your knight on e4 covers the last escape square. [%csl Re2] } (8... Kxd8 { Grabbing the bishop back is natural, and it throws away a mate in one. } 9. dxe4+ Ke7 { His pawn took your knight with check from his queen on d1. You are a queen down, and bishop to g4 was mate. [%cal Rd1d8] }) (8... Bb6 { The bishop retreats, and the mating net with it. } 9. dxe4 Bg4+ 10. Ke1 { His king slips back to e1, and you are a queen down. Bishop to g4 was mate a move ago. }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: oh no, my knight"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/qiKryhNu"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_0_5"]

{ The Stafford position once more: a pawn down, your pieces ready, his still at home. Your knight on f6 guards the centre. }
5. e5 { He pushes his pawn to e5 and chases your knight. Where does it go? Backwards is safe. Is there somewhere bolder? } 5... Ne4 { Straight to e4, into the middle of his camp. If he grabs it, you get something much bigger. [%csl Ge4] } (5... Nd5 { The safe central retreat, and completely sound. } 6. d4 c5 7. c3 { A normal position for you. But there is no trap here, and he is still a pawn up. }) (5... Ng8 { All the way home to g8. Safe, but two whole moves wasted. } 6. Bc4 Qd4 { Playable, but he develops with his bishop while your knight went backwards. }) 6. d3 { His d pawn kicks your knight on e4. Does the knight have to move? [%csl Re4] } 6... Bc5 { Ignore the attacked knight. Your bishop joins the attack on f2 instead. [%cal Gc5f2] } (6... Nc5 { The safe retreat, and he simply stands better once the knight backs away. } 7. d4 Nd7 { The knight is safe on d7, but it walked backwards twice and he has the centre. }) 7. dxe4 { He grabs the knight, and that is the losing choice. Trading bishops with his bishop to e3 was the move that survives. What now? [%cal Rc5f2] } 7... Bxf2+ { Check! A pawn falls, and his king must take the bishop. Look where that leaves his queen. } (7... Qxd1+ { You trade queens first, and the combination is gone. } 8. Kxd1 Bxf2 9. Nd2 { You are simply a piece down, with no mating ideas left. }) (7... Qh4 { Your queen hits f2, but it is not a check, and that gives him a whole move. } 8. Qf3 Bd4 { His queen guards f2, and you are a knight down for a pawn. [%cal Rf3f2] }) 8. Kxf2 { His king takes on f2. Now his queen on d1 faces yours down the open d column, and nothing stands between them. [%cal Rd8d1] } 8... Qxd1 { Oh no, his queen! You gave a knight and a bishop, and took his queen and two pawns back. } (8... Qh4+ { A real check this time, but it only lets his king run while his queen stays safe. } 9. Kg1 Qxe4 10. Qe2 { You gave a knight and a bishop for two pawns. His queen was the prize, and it walked away. }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: he does not take the knight"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/4n6UwK64"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p5/2b1P3/4n3/3P4/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 1 7"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p5/2b1P3/4n3/3P4/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_1_7"]

{ Your knight on e4 is attacked by his d pawn, and your bishop on c5 stares at f2. The trap is set. Will he take? }
7. Be3 { He does not take. His bishop to e3 is his best try: it blocks your bishop's line to f2. How do you answer? } 7... Bxe3 { Trade it off. When his f pawn takes back, his king loses its shield, and the road from h4 to e1 lies open. } (7... Nxf2 { A wild sacrifice, and he simply takes the knight. } 8. Kxf2 Bxe3+ 9. Kxe3 { The bishops came off, but your knight went for one pawn. You are a piece down. }) 8. fxe3 { His f pawn takes back. His king on e1 has no pawn on f2 in front of it any more. } 8... Qh4+ { Check! He can only block with the g pawn, or step to e2, where your bishop to g4 is mate. [%tail] } (8... Nc5 { The knight finally retreats, and the one moment for the check slips away. } 9. Qh5 Qe7 { His queen on h5 covers h4 now, and he stands better. [%cal Rh5h4] The check had to come at once. }) 9. g3 9... Nxg3 10. hxg3 10... Qxh1 { Your queen takes the rook in the corner. A whole rook for your knight: that was the check on h4. } 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: he grabs the centre"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/OPZzl8KT"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p5/4P3/4n3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 1 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p5/4P3/4n3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_1_6"]

{ Your knight has jumped to e4 in the middle of his camp, and his pawn stands on e5. His king side is still at home. }
6. d4 { He grabs central space with d4 instead of kicking your knight. Where is his weak spot, and who can reach it? } 6... Qh4 { Your queen swings out to h4 and stares straight at f2. [%cal Gh4f2] } (6... Be6 { A calm, normal developing move, just as good as the queen. } 7. c3 f5 { Solid chess. But the queen to h4 is the move that sets a trap. }) (6... f5 { You prop the knight up with a pawn. Reasonable, and slower. } 7. Be2 Be7 { He castles in peace next, and your attack never arrives. }) 7. g3 { He kicks your queen with the g pawn, and that is his mistake. His queen to f3, guarding f2, was the calm defence. Must your queen run? [%csl Rh4,Rf2] } 7... Nxg3 { A knight for a pawn, and his king side pawns are torn open. [%cal Gh4h1] } (7... Qh3 { The queen steps aside. But nothing concrete follows. } 8. Bg2 Nc5 { His bishop on g2 guards e4 and h1, and the attacking moment has passed. [%cal Rg2e4,Rg2h1] }) 8. fxg3 { He takes back with the f pawn. Now the e column and the long diagonal to h1 are both open. [%cal Ge4e1,Ge4h1] } 8... Qe4+ { Check! Your queen forks his king down the e column and his rook on h1 along the diagonal. [%cal Ge4e1,Ge4h1][%tail] } (8... Bb4+ { A check first looks clever, but it gives him time to tidy up. } 9. Kf2 Qe4 10. Bg2 { His bishop guards h1, his king is safe on f2, and you are a knight down. [%cal Rg2h1] }) 9. Be3 9... Qxh1 { The rook falls. You gave a knight for a pawn and took a whole rook back. } 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: the most common trap"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/rRL32xpq"]
[FEN "r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 0 5"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/8/4P3/8/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_0_5"]

{ The Stafford position: a pawn down, and every one of your pieces has a road toward his king. }
5. Nc3 { He develops his knight to c3 and guards e4 with it. Your plan does not change. [%cal Gc3e4] } 5... Bc5 { Your bishop lands on c5, staring down the diagonal at f2. [%cal Gc5f2] } (5... Be6 { A solid square, but from e6 the bishop does not touch his king. On c5 it bites. } 6. d3 Qe7 { Playable chess, and completely harmless to him. }) (5... Qe7 { Fine on its own. But the bishop is your sharpest attacker, and c5 is its square. } 6. d3 Be6 7. Be2 { A quiet position, and he stays a pawn up. }) 6. d3 { The most natural move on the board, and the most common mistake. Pawn to h3, to stop a knight jump, was the move that holds. Why? [%csl Rg4] } 6... Ng4 { Two of your pieces hit f2 at once, and only his king guards it. [%cal Gg4f2,Gc5f2] } (6... O-O { Sensible, and he simply stays a pawn ahead. } 7. Be2 Ng4 8. Bxg4 { His bishop on e2 takes your knight, and your attack has nothing left to bite on. }) 7. Be3 { His bishop to e3 blocks the line to f2. Which of your pieces takes it? } 7... Nxe3 { The knight takes the bishop. Now his f pawn must take back, and that pawn was his king's shield. } (7... Bxe3 { The other capture looks the same, and is not. } 8. fxe3 Nxe3 9. Qd2 { His queen hits your knight on e3, and you must spend a move saving it. His game holds together. }) 8. fxe3 { His f pawn takes back, and now the pawn on e3 stands alone. } 8... Bxe3 { Your bishop takes it. Material is level again, your bishop sits deep on e3, and his king can never castle short in safety. [%csl Ge3] } (8... Qg5 { Also strong, and worth about the same. } 9. Qf3 Bxe3 { You take the pawn a move later, and his queen has come to f3 to help. }) (8... Qh4+ { A check first, and about the same result. } 9. g3 Qg5 { The pawn on e3 is still hanging. Taking it at once was simpler. }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: the knight comes back"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/BxXuPwWK"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/2b5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 2 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/2b5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_2_6"]

{ His knight is out on c3, and your bishop on c5 aims at f2. His king is still in the middle. }
6. Bc4 { He brings his bishop to c4 instead of playing d3. Does your usual attack still work? } 6... Ng4 { Both your pieces hit f2 again. [%cal Gg4f2,Gc5f2] } (6... O-O { Safe castling, and the sharp moment slips away. } 7. f3 b5 8. Bb3 { His pawn on f3 takes g4 away from your knight, and he stays a pawn up. }) (6... Bxf2+ { The greedy check. His king takes, and you have given a bishop for a pawn. } 7. Kxf2 Qd4+ 8. Ke1 { His king steps back to e1, and you are a piece down for two pawns. }) 7. Qf3 { His queen jumps to f3, guarding f2 and hitting f7 with his bishop. Your knight on g4 is loose. Where does it go? [%csl Rf2,Rg4] } 7... Ne5 { Back to e5: it hits his queen and his bishop, and guards your pawn on f7. [%cal Ge5f3,Ge5c4,Ge5f7] } (7... O-O { Castling, and he only stands a little better. } 8. h3 Nf6 { The knight went to g4 and back to f6 for nothing, and the sharp part is over. }) (7... Bxf2+ { Tempting, but his king steps to e2 and nothing more follows. } 8. Ke2 O-O { A whole bishop given for one pawn. Not a trade worth making. }) 8. Qe2 { His queen steps back to e2. Your knight still hits his bishop. What joins the attack? } 8... Qh4 { Your queen and bishop both aim at f2, your knight on e5 still hits his bishop on c4, and his king has not castled. [%cal Gh4f2,Gc5f2,Ge5c4] } (8... Nxc4 { He would love that trade: your best attacker for a bishop. } 9. Qxc4 Qg5 10. Qf1 { [%cal Rf1f2] His queen on f1 guards f2, your attack is spent, and he is still a pawn up. }) (8... a5 { A useful little move, nearly as strong. } 9. Qf1 b5 { You keep an edge, but his queen reached f1 and guards f2. The queen to h4 was faster. [%cal Rf1f2] }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: the quiet moves are not safe"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/Ibwl11lm"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/2b5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R w KQkq - 2 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/2b5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_2_6"]

{ His knight is on c3, your bishop on c5 aims at f2, and his king side bishop has not moved yet. }
6. Be2 { He tucks his bishop on e2 and gives you no target. New idea: push your h pawn, so your rook behind it gets a road. [%cal Yh7h5] } 6... h5 { Your rook pawn walks forward, and the knight jump to g4 can never be answered by bishop takes. [%cal Gh8h5] } (6... Qd4 { Even stronger: the queen goes straight to the centre, hitting e4 and f2. } 7. O-O Nxe4 8. Bf3 { A good game for you, the same queen idea one move sooner. Today we learn the pawn push first. }) (6... Be6 { A normal developing move, and he stands better while you are a pawn down. } 7. d3 Qd6 { Nothing is happening, and he quietly keeps his extra pawn. }) 7. h3 { He stops your knight from jumping to g4. Now the queen has a central square: d4. [%cal Yd8d4] } 7... Qd4 { Into the centre she goes. Your queen hits e4 and f2 at once, and nothing can chase her away. [%cal Gd4e4,Gd4f2] } (7... Bd4 { The bishop wants that square too. But the queen is the piece that hits two things from there. } 8. d3 Qe7 { Solid enough, and he keeps his extra pawn. }) (7... h4 { Pushing the pawn on. Far too slow: he develops in peace. } 8. d3 Be6 { Nothing is happening, and you used a move for very little. }) 8. Rf1 { His only good defence: the rook guards f2. Castling lets your knight jump to g4, and d3 or f3 lose to a mate on f2. Take the pawn on e4. [%csl Rf2][%cal Yf6e4] } 8... Nxe4 { The pawn comes back, and material is level. If his knight takes on e4, your queen takes back and sits in the centre. } (8... Bb4 { Pinning his knight, but it gives him room to breathe. } 9. d3 Bxc3+ 10. bxc3 { He untangles with d3 and stands better. The pawn on e4 was the target. }) 0-1


[Event "Stafford Gambit: everyone falls for this one"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Role "exercise"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/6X11ftX9/tWZBMLsI"]
[FEN "r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/2b5/4P3/3P4/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 1 6"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk2r/ppp2ppp/2p2n2/2b5/4P3/3P4/PPP2PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_1_6"]

{ His pawn stands on d3, and your bishop sits on c5. Your rook on h8 is still asleep. }
6. Be2 { He hides his bishop on e2. How can your rook in the corner join the attack? } 6... h5 { The rook pawn walks up. If his bishop ever takes a knight on g4, the h column opens for your rook. [%cal Gh8h5] } (6... Ng4 { The natural jump at f2. His bishop takes it, and your queen wins it back. } 7. Bxg4 Qh4 8. Qf3 Qxg4 9. Qxg4 Bxg4 { Queens are off, the piece is back, and he is simply a pawn up in a quiet ending. }) (6... a6 { A slow pawn move, and you are still a pawn down. } 7. c3 Qe7 { He sits comfortably, and you have nothing to show for the gambit. }) 7. O-O { He castles, right into it. Now what? } 7... Ng4 { Your knight lands on g4, right in front of the h column you just opened. [%cal Gh5g4] } (7... h4 { Pushing the pawn on without the knight. Far too slow. } 8. c3 Be6 { He gets time to organise, and you are still a pawn down. }) 8. h3 { He attacks your knight with the h pawn. Must it move? } 8... Qd6 { Leave the knight! Your queen aims at h2, and the rook on h8 waits for the column to open. [%cal Gd6h2] } (8... Ne5 { You save the knight, and the attack disappears. } 9. Qd2 Be6 { You are a pawn down with nothing to show for the gambit. }) 9. hxg4 { He takes the knight, and that is the losing choice. Pushing his pawn to e5, to block your queen, was the move that holds. How do you take back? } 9... hxg4 { The pawn takes, and the h column is open for your rook. He has no defence against mate. [%cal Gh8h1,Gd6h2] } (9... Bxg4 { The natural recapture, and it throws the attack away. } 10. Nd2 O-O-O 11. Nc4 { His knight hits your queen, the h column is still shut, and you are a knight down. }) 10. e5 { He throws his e pawn in the way of your queen. Can you take it? } 10... Qxe5 { Your queen takes and still aims at h2. } (10... Bxf2+ { Also crushing, just slower. } 11. Rxf2 Qxe5 { You land in the same attack a move later, with his rook drawn onto f2. }) 11. Bh6 { He throws his bishop to h6 to block the column. Which piece takes it? } 11... Rxh6 { Your rook takes and stands on the open h column. } (11... gxh6 { The pawn takes, and your rook never reaches the column. } 12. Bxg4 Be6 13. Bxe6 { He has traded off your attackers, and the position is level. }) 12. Re1 { He makes room for his king on f1. Nothing helps him now. } 12... Qh2+ { Check! His king runs to f1, and the corner is yours. [%tail] } (12... Bxf2+ { This mates too, by a longer road. } 13. Kf1 Qe3 { Still winning, but the check on h2 was the fastest. }) 13. Kf1 13... Qh1# { Checkmate. Your queen cannot be touched: the rook on h6 guards her all the way down the h column. [%cal Gh6h1] } 0-1
